abstract:
We study collective tree exploration with subtree-size predictions. In this problem, a team of $k$ mobile agents needs to explore an unknown rooted tree as fast as possible, and whenever a new vertex $u$ is discovered, the team receives a prediction $\hat n_u$ of the number of nodes $n_u$ in the subtree rooted at $u$. We provide algorithmic guarantees under two error measures: the maximum error $E=\max_u|\widehat n_u-n_u|$ and the multiplicative distortion $μ= (\max_u\widehat n_u/n_u)(\max_u n_u/\widehat n_u)$. For the maximum error, we present a deterministic algorithm that explores any tree with $n$ nodes and depth $D$ in $O(\text{OPT}+E)$ rounds, where $\text{OPT} = Θ(n/k+D)$ is the offline benchmark, and we show that there is no deterministic algorithm that admits an $O(\text{OPT})+o(E)$ guarantee. For the multiplicative distortion, we present several algorithms that induce a competitive ratio in $\widetilde O(\min\{ \sqrtμ\log k,
  μ\log\log k+μ^{1.5}
  \})$, where the $\widetilde O(\cdot)$ may hide logarithmic factors in $μ$.

Given a rank-\(r\) matroid on \(n\) elements with nonnegative costs and a partition into safe and vulnerable elements, we seek a minimum-cost set that spans after every deletion of at most \(k\) selected vulnerable elements. We resolve several open questions posed by Bentert, Fomin, Golovach, and Morelle (ESA 2025) concerning weighted costs, nonuniform failures, and explicitly specified failure scenarios.
  To this end, we develop two compression methods. For matroids given by an independence oracle, weighted flat-avoidance representatives yield a bounded subset of the ground set containing an optimal solution and a \(2^{\mathcal{O}((r+k)^4)}n^{\mathcal{O}(1)}\)-time algorithm. This establishes fixed-parameter tractability for weighted costs and nonuniform failures simultaneously. For explicitly represented linear matroids, polynomial certificates allow us to compress families of partial solutions while preserving successful completions under all allowed failures. This yields a deterministic algorithm using \(2^{\mathcal{O}(kr(r+k)\log(r+1))}n^{\mathcal{O}(1)}\) field operations over arbitrary fields. Over fixed finite fields, a simpler reduction uses parallel classes.
  For explicitly listed failure scenarios, we obtain fixed-parameter algorithms parameterized by rank and the number of scenarios. In contrast, the size-budget decision version is W[1]-hard parameterized by rank and maximum scenario size, even on uniform matroids with two-element scenarios. \keywords{Matroids \and Fault tolerance \and Fixed-parameter algorithms \and Representative families}

We prove unconditionally that either NE contains a language requiring Boolean circuits of size at least $2^n/(2n)$, or $VP^0_{\mathbb{Z}}
e VNP^0_{\mathbb{Z}}$, a constant-free version of Valiant's conjecture over the integers. The Boolean alternative implies that $NP
ot\subseteq SIZE[n^k]$ for every constant $k\ge1$. Our proof gives a fine-grained variant of the connection between Polynomial Identity Testing (PIT) and circuit lower bounds established by Kabanets and Impagliazzo (2004). We show that the specific identity tests required by this argument can be performed over the integers without a derandomization assumption.
  Over a fixed finite field $\mathbb{F}$, we first give a deterministic $O(st^{d-1})$-time algorithm for PIT on size-$s$ circuits computing degree-$d$ polynomials in $t$ variables. We further show that, for any fixed $d\ge2$ and $\varepsilon>0$, improving this running time by a factor of $t^{\varepsilon}$, even nondeterministically, implies either the same Boolean lower bound or an arithmetic circuit lower bound of $t^{1+\varepsilon/2}$ for a polynomial family from $VNP_{\mathbb{F}}$. If nondeterministic PIT instead runs in time $s^{o(d)}t^{(1-\varepsilon)d}$ for infinitely many degrees $d$, then the arithmetic lower bound strengthens to $VP_{\mathbb{F}}
e VNP_{\mathbb{F}}$. Over the integers, we give a deterministic PIT algorithm for syntactically set-multilinear circuits running in time $s^{O(1)}t^{5d/6}$, which yields the unconditional result above.
  Combining the Kabanets-Impagliazzo argument with range avoidance, we show that an NP algorithm for PIT over $\mathbb{Z}$ gives a purely arithmetic win-win: either $VP_{\mathbb{Z}}
e VNP_{\mathbb{Z}}$, or constant-degree polynomials of maximum circuit complexity can be constructed in FNP.

